 Execution-based evidence is A-tier. Test results, console output. Show the exact test that now fails and passes, using pseudocode.
 
+### On the head
+
+Once the PR exists, `python3 ~/.claude/skills/recap-pr/facts.py <pr>` fills the block between the markers from its sources: the head's checks, the review passes recorded on it, the PRs a base merge brought in, and any facts the repo declares. Rerun it to refresh the block rather than editing it. Elsewhere in the body, point to the block for its figures rather than retyping them.
+
 ### Merge Danger
 
 Describe whether it's a one-way or two-way door. You can walk back through two-way doors, but not one-way doors. A PR that is cheap to roll back is lower risk. Changes that involve destructive actions or hard-to-reverse decisions are one-way doors.
 
 The blast radius is the potential impact or scope of the changes introduced by this PR. Consider all possibilities. Examples are layout shift, breakages for consumers, mobile responsiveness, etc.
+
+## Editing the body
+
+Edit a PR description from its live text: fetch it (`gh pr view <n> --json body -q .body`) just before the edit, change only the section you mean to, and keep every other section as it is, bots' summaries and other tools' blocks included. Read it back after writing: a review bot can rewrite the body from an older copy just after a push, so reapply your change on the latest body when it is gone, up to three times, then report that a bot keeps reverting it.
